CMP(1)                         P32W Unix                          CMP(1)

NAME
       cmp - compare two files

SYNOPSIS
       cmp [-l | -s] file1 file2

DESCRIPTION
       cmp compares the two files byte by byte. If they are the same
       it prints nothing. Otherwise it prints the position of the
       first difference, counting bytes and lines from 1:

              a b differ: char 1037, line 42

       If one file is a leading part of the other it says

              cmp: EOF on a

       naming the shorter file. Both messages go to the standard
       output. A file named - is the standard input.

OPTIONS
       -l     list every difference, not just the first: for each
              differing byte its number (from 1, decimal) and the two
              bytes in octal, as in

                   1037 141 142

              then cmp: EOF on name if the lengths differ
       -s     silent: print nothing for files that differ; only the
              exit status tells (errors are still reported)

EXAMPLES
       cmp old.bin new.bin
       if cmp -s a b; then echo same; fi
       cmp -l old.bin new.bin | wc -l   how many bytes changed

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the files are identical; 1 if they differ; 2 if a file
       could not be opened or for a usage error (an unknown option,
       not two files, or -l with -s).

SEE ALSO
       od(1), test(1)

BUGS
       There are no skip offsets. There is no diff command.
